Similarity concerns the graded comparison of concepts according to how alike they are ( Tversky, 1977 ) . Geometry ( ✓ ) provides a canonical account of similarity through metrics, distances, angles, and transformations, and is therefore naturally suited to this operation ( Do Carmo, 2016 ; Stillwell et al., 2005 ) , while information theory ( ∘ \bm{\circ} ) provides closely related measures of distinguishability such as Kullback-Leibler and Jensen-Shannon divergence ( Cover et al., 1991 ) . Probability ( ∘ \bm{\circ} ) supports graded similarity through likelihoods and distances or divergences between probability distributions ( Billingsley, 2012 ) ; graphs ( ∘ \bm{\circ} ) can support similarity through graph distances, shared neighborhoods, or structural equivalence ( Lorrain and White, 1971 ) ; algebra ( ∘ \bm{\circ} ) through relations or metrics defined on algebraic structures ( Cohen and Welling, 2016 ) ; category theory ( ∘ \bm{\circ} ) through structure-preserving mappings ( Mac Lane and MacLane, 1971 ) ; and dynamical systems ( ∘ \bm{\circ} ) through distances between states or trajectories ( Shannon, 1948 ) . In each case, however, similarity is derived from the framework’s underlying structure rather than being a primary object of the framework (
